{
  "name": "v10_op_farm",
  "description": "fnet v10 component 3: the always-on op farm - two hardwired vec-vec operand pairs (A = VREG0 x VREG1 with MUL/SUB/MAX/MIN/DIV/MOD, B = VREG2 x VREG3 with MUL/SUB plus the SQUARE block) and the vec-scal block (VREG0 against the memory-mapped $vec_bcast row: MUL/ADD/SUB/DIV/MOD and the GT/LT escape masks). Each result is read by parking $vec_rsel on its mux index, exercising the op and its select gate together. Indices are BLOCKED with reserves - registers 1..16, pair A 17..24, pair B 25..32, vec-scal 33..44, generators and I/O 45..52 - so adding a unit renumbers nothing. A_MIN and VS_LT are here so a comparison and its complement are each one op rather than a mask plus an all-ones subtract. THE SQUARE BLOCK squares one register against its own opposite face (the bank exports both faces of VREG2 and VREG3), which keeps it to the ordinary each[red] x each[green] shape; with zx in VREG2 and zy in VREG3 one pair load then yields zx*zy, zx^2, zy^2, zx^2-zy^2 and - by selecting B_SQ and B_SQG together so the frames sum - zx^2+zy^2, i.e. every quantity a mandelbrot iteration needs including its escape test. FOUR SEMANTICS ARE PINNED HERE rather than assumed. (1) each-vs-each ops iterate the UNION of both input networks, a lane missing on one side reading 0: $mem[3] (red only) and $mem[4] (green only) both survive into SUB and MAX while MUL drops them (n*0=0) and MIN collapses them (min(n,0)=0), so MAX and MIN are not symmetric on ragged operands - so operand lane sets must be kept aligned, MUL being self-cleaning and SUB not. (2) each-vs-FIXED-signal ops iterate only the first signal's networks, which is why the vec-scal units can read the whole scalar memory bus for their broadcast without dragging every control row in as a junk lane - asserted by checking $vec_rsel and $vec_bcast are absent from a vec-scal result. (3) integer DIVISION truncates toward zero, not toward negative infinity, and (4) MODULO takes the sign of the dividend - both measured here because the mandelbrot kernel divides signed products by its fixed-point scale every iteration and a Python reference model has to match Factorio exactly.",
